SCOPE -- WHAT THIS MIT LICENSE COVERS
------------------------------------

The MIT License above covers everything in this repository EXCEPT the Ars Magica
rulebook material carved out below. That includes the Rust crates (`crates/`),
the Svelte/TypeScript frontend (`ui/`), the Fluent UI strings (`locales/`), the
sample saves (`examples/`), the extraction and helper tooling (`scripts/`), the
documentation (`docs/`, `.github/`), and the build configuration and scripts.


THE ARS MAGICA RULEBOOK MARKDOWN -- CC BY-SA 4.0
-----------------------------------------------

The rulebook text under `rules/source/` is NOT covered by the MIT License. It is
derived from copyrighted material owned by Trident, Inc. d/b/a Atlas Games and is
redistributed in full under the Ars Magica Open License, i.e. the Creative
Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License ("CC BY-SA 4.0"):

    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

See `rules/source/LICENSE` for the attribution, trademark and share-alike terms.


THE RULES JSON -- DUAL LICENSED, BY FORM AND BY CONTENT
------------------------------------------------------

The generated rules data in `rules/core/` and `rules/i18n/` holds two separable
bodies of work, and they are licensed differently:

  * THE FORM is MIT. The JSON schema, the object and key names, the stable
    slug-identifier scheme, the file layout and ordering conventions, and all of
    the language-neutral mechanics in `rules/core/` -- numbers, enums,
    prerequisite and grant structures, and the citations back to the source text
    -- were authored for this project and are covered by the MIT License above.
    `rules/core/` contains no rulebook text at all; where a slug echoes a
    rulebook term it is used as an identifier, not as expressive content.

  * THE CONTENT is CC BY-SA 4.0. The human-readable rules text carried in the
    string values of `rules/i18n/<lang>/` -- the spell, Virtue/Flaw, Ability,
    Art, House and equipment names, descriptions, summaries and specialties --
    is extracted from the rulebooks and is in places verbatim rulebook prose. It
    is derived expressive content and carries the same CC BY-SA 4.0 terms as the
    Markdown it came from. Translating that text produces a derivative, which is
    likewise CC BY-SA 4.0.

In plain terms: reusing the FORMAT -- writing your own data against this schema,
or building tooling on it -- needs only the MIT License. Redistributing or
adapting the Ars Magica rules TEXT keeps it under CC BY-SA 4.0 with attribution
preserved.

See `rules/core/LICENSE` and `rules/i18n/LICENSE` for the per-directory
statement, and `rules/source/LICENSE` for the full CC BY-SA 4.0 terms.


COMBINING THE TWO
-----------------

Distributing the CC BY-SA 4.0 rules content alongside the MIT-licensed
application in a single installer or archive is a collection, not an adaptation.
It does not place the application code under CC BY-SA 4.0.

Ars Magica is a trademark of Trident, Inc. d/b/a Atlas Games. This is an
unofficial fan tool and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Atlas Games.
